 and leg","methodDirectNodeIds":{"hl":["cnm_125","cnm_121"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_125","cnm_121","cnm_064"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:d80f5241f43281e1c465d17d"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C (equal hypotenuse and one leg)","reason":"C gives the hypotenuse and one leg, the HL pattern. A gives SAS using the right angle; B and D supply angle-side criteria together with the equal right angles.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"cn-math","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_125"],"coverage":"partial","flags":["'Requires using HL' is interpreted as identifying the direct given-data criterion, not excluding a longer Pythagorean reduction to SSS.","Interpret requires HL as directly applicable from the current givens, not as uniqueness after all possible auxiliary derivations."],"questionId":"cnm_125_q3","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_110","cnm_120"],"id":"compare-direct-criteria","label":"Compare directly applicable criteria","methodDirectNodeIds":{"compare-direct-criteria":["cnm_125","cnm_123","cnm_124"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_125","cnm_123","cnm_124","cnm_110","cnm_120"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"alignmentIds":["alignment:d80f5241f43281e1c465d17d"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"6","reason":"The right triangles BCD and ADC are congruent by HL, giving AD=BC=6. Equivalently, AD²=AC²−CD²=BD²−CD²=BC².","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_125"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_125_q5","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"id":"common-leg-hl","label":"HL with common leg CD","nodeIds":["cnm_125","cnm_121"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_120"],"id":"solution:common-leg-hl:ef24620920e1","label":"HL with the common leg","methodDirectNodeIds":{"common-leg-hl":["cnm_125","cnm_121"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_125","cnm_121","cnm_120"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:d80f5241f43281e1c465d17d"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 4.0","reason":"The perpendicular projections of A and B leave equal overhangs of (10-4)/2=3. Thus B's foot is 7 units from D. Since B is the midpoint of DE, its horizontal coordinate is half that of E, giving DF=14 and CF=14-10=4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_125"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"1989_AMC_12_Q04","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"congruence-and-midsegments","label":"Projection congruence and midpoint theorem","methodDirectNodeIds":{"congruence-and-midsegments":["cnm_125","cnm_186","cnm_185"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_125","cnm_186","cnm_185"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p077","cnm_p105","cnm_064","cnm_067","cnm_110"],"id":"perpendicular-feet-and-midpoint","label":"Use perpendicular feet and the midpoint condition","methodDirectNodeIds":{"perpendicular-feet-and-midpoint":["cnm_055","cnm_125","cnm_121","cnm_185"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_055","cnm_125","cnm_121","cnm_185","cnm_p077","cnm_p105","cnm_064","cnm_067","cnm_110"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:d80f5241f43281e1c465d17d"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, x=100 degrees","reason":"Extending each trapezoid's legs gives an isosceles wedge with vertex angle 180-2(180-x)=2x-180. The nine congruent wedges span 180 degrees, so 9(2x-180)=180. Thus x=100 degrees.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_125"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2009_AMC_10B_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_069","cnm_125"],"id":"solution:outer-ten-gon-angle-sum:be328c391df7","label":"Count the angles of the outer polygon","methodDirectNodeIds":{"outer-ten-gon-angle-sum":["cnm_058","cnm_181"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_058","cnm_181","cnm_069","cnm_125"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_069","cnm_125","cnm_056"],"id":"equal-leg-direction-turns","label":"Divide the half-turn among the trapezoids","methodDirectNodeIds":{"equal-leg-direction-turns":["cnm_058","cnm_115"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_058","cnm_115","cnm_069","cnm_125","cnm_056"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:d80f5241f43281e1c465d17d"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 24/5","reason":"Triangles ABC and ABD are congruent right triangles with hypotenuse AB. Their perpendiculars to AB have the same foot H and length CH=DH=(3*4)/5=12/5. Since CD^2=CH^2+DH^2, CH is perpendicular to DH. Together with DH perpendicular to AB, this makes DH a height above ABC. Thus V=(1/3)*6*(12/5)=24/5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_125"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2015_AMC_10A_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"common-hypotenuse-height","label":"Find a perpendicular tetrahedron height","methodDirectNodeIds":{"common-hypotenuse-height":["cnm_125","cnm_121","cnm_h120"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_125","cnm_121","cnm_h120"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"alignmentIds":["alignment:d80f5241f43281e1c465d17d"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"55°","reason":"AB=DE are hypotenuses and AC=DF are corresponding legs, so HL gives ∠D=∠A=35°. Hence ∠E=90°−35°=55°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_125"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_125_q4","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_120"],"id":"hl-and-complement","label":"HL and complementary acute angles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"hl-and-complement":["cnm_125","cnm_121","cnm_116"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_125","cnm_121","cnm_116","cnm_120"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:d80f5241f43281e1c465d17d"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, II and III only","reason":"Write DE=2d and its altitude as h, with common leg length r. Then d²+h²=r². Since AB=2h, its altitude is d. Both areas are dh, and the half-apex angles are complementary, so the full apex angles sum to 180°.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_125"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"1976_AMC_12_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"id":"congruent-right-triangle-halves","label":"Congruent right-triangle halves","nodeIds":["cnm_125","cnm_121","cnm_116"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_112","cnm_055","cnm_059"],"id":"solution:congruent-right-triangle-halves:9672a4e371d9","label":"Compare the right-triangle halves","methodDirectNodeIds":{"congruent-right-triangle-halves":["cnm_137","cnm_125","cnm_121","cnm_116","cnm_p148"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_137","cnm_125","cnm_121","cnm_116","cnm_p148","cnm_112","cnm_055","cnm_059"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"alignmentIds":["alignment:d80f5241f43281e1c465d17d"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 2","reason":"Normalize the square side to 1 and let AE = t. The right triangles ABE and BCF are congruent, so CF = t and DE = DF = 1-t. Equilateral side equality gives 1+t² = 2(1-t)², hence (1-t)² = 2t. Therefore [DEF]/[ABE] = ((1-t)²/2)/(t/2) = 2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_125"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"2004_AMC_10A_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"congruence-and-pythagoras","label":"Congruence and Pythagoras","methodDirectNodeIds":{"congruence-and-pythagoras":["cnm_125","cnm_121","cnm_174"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_125","cnm_121","cnm_174"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:d80f5241f43281e1c465d17d"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 3/5","reason":"Let the larger base and diagonal be L, and the smaller base and altitude be s. Then L^2=s^2+((L+s)/2)^2. With r=s/L, this gives 5r^2+2r-3=(5r-3)(r+1)=0, so r=3/5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_125"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"1953_AMC_12_Q48","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_121","cnm_040"],"id":"diagonal-and-difference-of-squares","label":"Find the diagonal's horizontal length","methodDirectNodeIds":{"diagonal-and-difference-of-squares":["cnm_174","cnm_125","cnm_152","cnm_042"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_174","cnm_125","cnm_152","cnm_042","cnm_121","cnm_040"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"alignmentIds":["alignment:d80f5241f43281e1c465d17d"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 3 lines","reason":"Put A = (0,0), B = (5,0), and write a line as ux + vy = 2 with u squared + v squared = 1. The second distance requires |5u - 2| = 3, so u = 1 or -1/5. These give one and two lines respectively, totaling three.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_125"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"1990_AMC_12_Q06","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_064","cnm_067"],"id":"side-cases-and-parallel-shift","label":"Separate the two side cases","methodDirectNodeIds":{"side-cases-and-parallel-shift":["cnm_065","cnm_072","cnm_174","cnm_125"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_065","cnm_072","cnm_174","cnm_125","cnm_064","cnm_067"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"alignmentIds":["alignment:d80f5241f43281e1c465d17d"],"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 80 square units","reason":"Let the shorter base be b and each leg be l. Tangency gives 16+b = 2l. Since sin θ = 0.8 and cos θ = 0.6, 16−b = 2l cos θ = 1.2l. These give l = 10, b = 4, and height 8. The area is (16+4)×8/2 = 80.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","canonicalNodeIds":["cnm_125"],"coverage":"partial","flags":[],"questionId":"1988_AMC_12_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["math:inverse-sine-cosine-principal-branches","cnm_125","cnm_090"],"id":"tangent-lengths-and-leg-projections","label":"Combine tangency with right triangles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"tangent-lengths-and-leg-projections":["cnm_263","cnm_276","cnm_h062","cnm_p149"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_263","cnm_276","cnm_h062","cnm_p149","math:inverse-sine-cosine-principal-branches","cnm_125","cnm_090"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":8,"alignmen